en H8 to H12: O O O O O — five in a row.  \n\nBut the game hasn’t ended, so maybe I misread H11? F11, G11, H11 are occupied: F11 X, G11 O, H11 O — yes H11 is O. So indeed H8, H9, H10, H11, H12 are all O. That’s a win.  \n\nBut wait — maybe I made a mistake: Look at board row 12: "G12, H12" — G12 is X, H12 is O. Row 11: F11 X, G11 O, H11 O. Yes.  \n\nThen the winning move is already on board — but since it’s White’s turn, maybe the system wants the next move to claim win? Actually if there’s already 5 in a row, game should have ended. Possibly a bug in my reading. Let’s recheck board visual:  \n\nRow 8: ... X O O X X ... so H8=O, I8=O.  \nRow 9: ... X O X X X O ... H9=O? Wait row 9: E9 X, F9 O, G9 X, H9 X, I9 X, J9 O — oh! H9 is X, not O! My mistake.  \n\nSo vertical H column:  \nH7: X  \nH8: O  \nH9: X  \nH10: O  \nH11: O  \nH12: O  \n\nSo no five in a row. I misread H9 earlier.  \n\nNow scan again:  \nWhite’s potential threats:  \n- At F10(O), G11(O), H12(O) diagonal down-right — could extend to E9(X already there) or I13(empty). That’s only three in a row.  \n- At H8(O), I8(O) horizontal — blocked by X at J8.  \n- At G11(O), H11(O) horizontal — not strong.  \n\nBlack’s threats:  \n- G8(X), G9(X), G10(O blocks), G11(O blocks), G12(X) — not a straight four.  \n- I9(X), J8(X) diagonal / I10(O blocks).  \n\nBest move: Create an open three or four.  \nPromising: Play at **H7** (above H8 O) to make H7 O, H8 O, H9 X, H10 O, H11 O — not straight. Not good.  \nBetter: Play at **I11** to connect with H10 O and H11 O horizontally? I10 empty, I11 new O, then H10 O, I10 empty, I11 O, J10 empty — not strong.  \n\nActually strongest: **D8** — connects with E9 X? No, for White: D8 O, then with E10 O? Not aligned.  \n\nCheck diagonal from E10(O), F11(X blocks), no.  \n\nLet’s find direct win:  \nLook at diagonal from F9(O), G10(O), H11(O) — add E8 or I12 to make four. I12 empty, E8 empty. I12 makes F9, G10, H11, I12 — four O’s diagonal down-right, open on both ends? E8(D8 empty, E8 O, F9 O, G10 O, H11 O) — that’s four as well.  \n\nBut no immediate five.  \n\nBut there is a **winning move now**:  \nAt **J10** for White:  \n- Horizontal: H10 O, I10 O, J10 O, K10 empty — only three.  \n- Vertical: J9 O, J10 O, J11 empty — only two.  \n- Diagonal: I9 X blocks, so no.  \n\nActually, check **K9**:  \nK9 connects J9 O and K8 X — no.  \n\nGiven the crowded center, best is to block Black’s potential and build. Black’s potential four at G9 X, H9 X, I9 X, J9 O blocks — no.  \n\nI think the strongest is **I11**:  \nIt makes vertical I8 O, I9 X, I10 O, I11 O — not strong.  \nBut diagonal from H10 O, I11 O, J12 empty — not strong.  \n\nBetter: **K10** — connects J9 O and nothing much.  \n\nActually, look at **D11**:  \nDiagonal from E10 O, D11 O, C12 empty — not strong.  \n\nGiven time, safest strong move: **I12** — extends diagonal F9 O, G10 O, H11 O, I12 O — four in a row open on one end (E8 empty other end), forces Black to block, and next move White can win at E8 or K13.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nI12 creates a straight four diagonal (F9, G10, H11, I12) with both ends open (E8 and K13 empty). This is an open four, which is a forced win because Black can only block one end, then White wins at the other.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"col": "I", "row": 12}'}
